state local governments have made  trillions of dollars of promises to  their employees that they will pay them  pensions when those employees retire and  they've budgeted for these using methods  that are basically flawed in essence  what state local governments do is they  assume that for every dollar they set  aside that that dollar is going to grow  at an 8% compound annualized return they  assume that every dollar they set aside  is going to double in nine years and  that leads to budgeting procedures that  that greatly understate the costs of of  providing pensions to public sector  employees what this means is that there  is really no state or local government  around the u.s. that has run a balanced  budget in a very long time if state and  local governments do not achieve the  returns that they're targeting taxpayers  will be asked to contribute more to the  to the state local governments into  pension systems in order to pay for the  retirements of public sector employees  that's already happening now and it's  creating a lot of tension between the  10% of the u.s. population that works  for a state or local government and the  90% that does not so essentially we have  a situation where 10% of the population  is owed four and a half trillion dollars  by another 90 percent of the population  that 10% of the population happens to be  employees of state local governments who  provide essential services to us on a  daily basis it's actually hard to  imagine a worse arrangement than  borrowing sums of money that we are  never going to be able to pay back from  people who provide essential services to  us every day  like teachers and policemen firemen  Public Safety officials and other public  employees  financial markets don't like uncertainty  and in fact this is one of the reasons  that I see the financial crisis that  state and local governments in the US  will experience in the next decade or so  as being very analogous to the crisis  that is being experienced in Europe  indeed even if you think about Greece  which is not a large country by by  European standards the uncertainty over  how that Greek debt was going to get  restructured through financial markets  into a lot of turmoil and in the u.s.  there's a great deal of uncertainty as  to how all of these unfunded debts are  going to get resolved because laws in  many states offer protections to these  public employee pensions and as a result  the states are not going to be able to  simply default on these pensions there's  going to be great a great deal of  competition between municipal bond  holders and the recipients of the of the  pensions as to who is going to get paid  first state and local government pension  funds are one of the main investors in  private equity and venture capital in  fact one could say that they are really  the lifeblood of private equity and  venture capital firms they provide  almost four hundred billion dollars of  current investments and commitments to  private equity venture capital and  related investments and a lot of these  funds are now looking to be cash flow  negative the amount of assets that they  have is decreasing as these pension  funds come under greater and greater  strains and right now you have two  things going on on the one hand they  have the incentive to maintain these  investments in somewhat riskier asset  classes in order to target expected  returns but on the other hand the amount  of assets under management is going down  and I think that for the private equity  and venture capital industries it's  going to be important to have a robust  source of financing that's going to  continue from public sector pension  funds and so the private equity and  venture capital industries will need to  be thinking about where the funding  sources for their investments are going  to be in five to ten years who are going  to be the main limited partners for  their investments in the next five to  ten years because the traditional  lifeblood of public sector pension funds  may or may not still be there on a  medium to long term horizon
 

